(
 '## Overview'
 ''
 'This package comes with functions and operators that allow you to prompt the user, and make her...'
 ''
 '* answer simple questions like "Are you sure?" or "Do you really want to delete this file?" (`YesOrNo`)'
 '* select an item (or multiple items) from a list of items, or quit (`Select`)'
 '* stop and read a message before she can continue (`Pause`)'
 '* enter a character vector in response to something like "Enter your name:" (`AskForText`)'
 '* enter a number in response to something like "How many copies? " (`AskForNumber`)'
 ''
 'This is useful for interacting with the user in the session, something that user commands do quite often.'
 ''
 'Note that although `AskForNumber` and `AskForText` are operators, when this document refers to '
 '"the `CommTools` functions", these two operators are included. '
 ''
 '## Additional features'
 ''
 '### Interrupting the functions'
 ''
 'When a user is prompted for input, due to a long-standing bug in Dyalog you can issue neither a weak nor a '
 'strong interrupt. Answering the question or selecting an item and then trying to issue an interrupt does not '
 'work well on modern machines because they are just too fast.'
 ''
 'That''s why all functions allow you to enter `∘∘∘`, which makes the functions run on a stop vector that '
 'is dynamically set for just this purpose.'
 ''
 '### Automated feedback'
 ''
 '#### Why automate?'
 ''
 'Imagine you want to implement a test suite for a set of user commands that use `CommTools` for communicating '
 'with the user. With `CommTools` you can automate this, so that your tests don''t require a human in front of'
 'the monitor.'
 ''
 '#### How to automate'
 ''
 'This can be achieved by assigning data to a global variable `∆Automation`. Usually this doesn''t exist, and it '
 'is not required by `CommTools`. There is a helper `AddAutomation` available that makes this simple.'
 ''
 'The variable has four columns:'
 ''
 '1. The type: one of `AskForNumber`, `AskForText`, `Pause`, `Select` or `YesOrNo`'
 '2. The ID: either the message/question/caption itself, or an alias'
 '3. The result to be returned in case of a match'
 '4. A counter: how many times this row was triggered'
 ''
 'The process is easy to explain in an example. Let''s assume you want to ask a user whether a file should really'
 'be deleted.'
 ''
 '```'
 '      AddAutomation ''YesOrNo'' ''Sure?'' ''y'''
 '      ListAutomation '''''
 ' Type     ID     Returns  Counter '
 ' ----     --     -------  ------- '
 ' YesOrNo  Sure?  y              0 '
 '      YesOrNo ''Sure?'''
 ('1'⋄)
 '      YesOrNo ''Really sure?''   ⍝ Not on ∆Automation'
 ''
 'Really sure? (y/n) y'
 ('1'⋄)
 '```'
 ''
 'Note that `AddAutomation` has a shy result, which is why nothing is printed to the session in the example.'
 ''
 'When no row of `∆Automation` matches, the function falls back to asking the user, as `YesOrNo ''Really sure?'''
 'demonstrates. Automation is therefore always optional, and never gets in your way.'
 ''
 '#### Aliases'
 ''
 'In very simple cases, using the question/message/caption as identifier might suffice, but generally you are'
 'advised to use an alias instead.'
 ''
 'If the question/message/caption is created dynamically, you even MUST use an alias, because you cannot know'
 'upfront what the question/message/caption eventually will be.'
 ''
 'An example is the question "Are you sure that you want to delete the file /path/2/file/log-2014-11-11?"'
 ''
 'Let''s replace the entry with an alias:'
 ''
 '```'
 '      AddAutomation ''YesOrNo'' ''DeleteLogFile@'' ''y'''
 '      ListAutomation '''''
 ' Type     ID              Returns  Counter '
 ' ----     --              -------  ------- '
 ' YesOrNo  DeleteLogFile@  y              0 '
 '      YesOrNo ''DeleteLogFile@Sure?'''
 ('1'⋄)
 '```'
 ''
 'The `@`-character defines it as an alias. This will work no matter what the actual filename is. Note that an'
 'alias **must not** contain spaces, and that it must be the *first* word of the question/message/caption. The'
 'alias itself is removed before the question/message/caption is shown to the user.'
 ''
 'Note also that you can add a kind of comment:'
 ''
 '```'
 '      AddAutomation ''YesOrNo'' ''DeleteLogFile@My Comment'' ''y'''
 '```'
 ''
 'This can be used to explain what a specific row in `∆Automation` is good for. Everything after the `@` is'
 'ignored when a match is attempted.'
 ''
 '#### Logging of automated responses '
 ''
 'Note that whenever the automation is triggered, the counter in the last (fourth) column of `∆Automation` is '
 'increased. This can be used to check whether an intended automation actually took place. '
 ''
 '#### Cleaning up'
 ''
 'Of course there is nothing wrong with deleting the global variable `∆Automation` yourself. However, you can '
 'also just call the function `CommTools.Cleanup`, which will do the job for you.'
 ''
 'This has a small advantage: if `CommTools` is extended in the future, `Cleanup` might do more than that, so '
 'calling it is safer than trying to do the job yourself. '
 ''
 'Note that `Cleanup` does not remove a function established with `SetOnWait`, see [Getting notified](#).'
 ''
 '### Getting notified'
 ''
 'A user command that runs for a while tends to be left alone: the user switches to something else, and when'
 'the user command eventually asks a question, nobody notices that the session is waiting for input.'
 ''
 'That''s why you can establish a function that is called whenever a `CommTools` function is about to wait for'
 'input from the user. That function may then notify the user in whatever way suits your platform, for example'
 'with a toast notification on Windows, `notify-send` on Linux or `osascript` on macOS.'
 ''
 '```'
 '      #.Notify CommTools.SetOnWait 0'
 '```'
 ''
 'The right argument is ignored; pass a 0.'
 ''
 'The function must be monadic or ambivalent, and it must return a result, which is ignored. It is called'
 'monadically with a two-item vector as right argument:'
 ''
 '1. The type: one of `AskForNumber`, `AskForText`, `Pause`, `Select` or `YesOrNo`'
 '2. The question; for `Pause` the message, and for `Select` the caption, which might be empty. An alias is'
 '   removed.'
 ''
 '```'
 '∇ r←Notify(type question)'
 '  r←⍬'
 '  ⍝ Start a notifier of your choice here, but do not wait for it'
 '∇'
 '```'
 ''
 'Notes:'
 ''
 '* The function is called only when the user is really asked: when automation answers a question, it is'
 '  not called.'
 '* It is called once per question, even when the user''s input is invalid and the question is asked again.'
 '* The question is shown to the user only after the function has returned, so it should not wait for'
 '  the notifier to finish.'
 '* Errors are not trapped: when the function fails, the `CommTools` function fails as well.'
 '* Use a tradfn: when you change a dfn after having called `SetOnWait`, Dyalog versions prior to 21.0 keep'
 '  calling the old definition until you call `SetOnWait` again.'
 '* The function is established in the `CommTools` namespace that `SetOnWait` was called on; any other copy of'
 '  `CommTools` in the workspace or in `⎕SE` is not affected.'
 ''
 '`SetOnWait` returns a shy result: 1 if it replaced a function established earlier, and 0 otherwise.'
 ''
 '`UnsetOnWait` removes the function. It returns a shy result: 1 if there was a function to be removed, and 0'
 'otherwise.'
 ''
 'Note that `Cleanup` does not remove the function: test suites typically call `Cleanup`, and that should not'
 'switch your notifications off.'
 ''
 '## The main functions'
 ''
 'Note that the reference section does not discuss automation except when something out of the ordinary needs '
 'to be mentioned (`Pause`, `Select`).'
 ''
 '### `YesOrNo`'
 ''
 'Imagine asking this question:'
 ''
 '```'
 '      CommTools.YesOrNo ''Are you sure you want to delete this file?'''
 '```'
 ''
 'The function accepts "y", "Y", "n" and "N" as answers, and keeps asking until it gets one of them. It returns'
 'a 1 for "yes" and a 0 for "no".'
 ''
 'Note that leading spaces in the question are removed, and that a question mark is appended in case the'
 'question does not contain one already.'
 ''
 '#### Specifying a default'
 ''
 'By default the user must answer the question: pressing `<enter>` without entering anything achieves nothing.'
 ''
 'You may change this by passing a Boolean as left argument: it defines what is returned when the user just'
 'presses `<enter>`. The prompt reflects the default by capitalizing it:'
 ''
 '```'
 '      1 CommTools.YesOrNo ''Continue?''     ⍝ (Y/n) --- <enter> means "yes"'
 '      0 CommTools.YesOrNo ''Continue?''     ⍝ (y/N) --- <enter> means "no"'
 '```'
 ''
 '#### Multiple-line questions                    '
 ''
 '`YesOrNo` accepts nested vectors as questions as well as a character vector with `⎕UCS 10` in it. (Note that '
 'a nested vector is internally converted to a simple character vector with `⎕UCS 10` (Line Feed) in it).'
 ''
 'If the question contains `⎕UCS 13` (Carriage Return), then those will be converted into `⎕UCS 10`.'
 ''
 'Because a multiple-line question is hard to specify as an ID, you are strongly advised to use an alias when'
 'you want to automate such a question.'
 ''
 '### `Select` '
 ''
 'This function allows you to present a list of up to 999 choices to the user. She might then select a single one '
 'or none (that''s the default), or multiple ones/none, or a particular number of them, or all, or quit. '
 ''
 'The right argument must be a vector of character vectors. Note that leading spaces in choices are removed.'
 ''
 'The result is:'
 ''
 '* a scalar (the number of the selected item) if exactly one item was selected'
 '* a vector of item numbers if more than one item was selected'
 '* `⍬` if the user pressed `<enter>` without selecting anything (only allowed when `mustFlag` is not 1)'
 '* ¯1 in case the user entered "q" (for quit); only allowed when `quitFlag` is 1, see below'
 ''
 'Example:'
 ''
 '```'
 '      l←⊂''First'''
 '      l,←⊂''Second'''
 '      l,←⊂''Third'''
 '      caption←''Please select hot item:'''
 '      caption CommTools.Select l  '
 '``` '
 ''
 'The left argument takes up to four positional parameters: `caption`, `manyFlag`, `mustFlag` and `quitFlag`.'
 ''
 'If you want to allow the user to select multiple items, pass a 1 as second positional parameter:'
 ''
 '```   '
 '      caption 1 CommTools.Select l  '
 '```'
 ''
 'The user may then enter several item numbers at once (separated by spaces), or an "a" for "all". She may also'
 'enter `a~{n}`, which is treated as "all but n", where n is one or more item numbers. This is particularly'
 'helpful when the list is long and she wants all of them but a few.'
 ''
 'Duplicates are ignored: entering `2 2` selects item 2 just once.'
 ''
 'Ranges save typing: `3-6` stands for `3 4 5 6`, and `1 2 5 10-13 17` for `1 2 5 10 11 12 13 17`. Commas are'
 'treated like spaces, so `1,2,5` works as well. A range must not contain any spaces, so `3 - 6`, `3 -6` and'
 '`3- 6` are rejected, and its second number must be greater than the first one, so `5-5` is rejected, too.'
 'Ranges may also follow `a~`: `a~10-40` selects all items but 10 to 40.'
 ''
 'Ranges are on offer whenever more than one item may be selected, and that includes the `(min max)` syntax.'
 ''
 '#### Getting help'
 ''
 'The prompt lists none of this syntax; it ends with `(Get help with ?)`. When the user enters a `?`, the'
 'choices are put on display again with the help injected just above the input line.'
 ''
 'Only what the particular call allows is mentioned: a user who may not quit is not told about "q", and one'
 'who may select just a single item is not told about ranges, "a" and "a~n".'
 ''
 'Sometimes the user must select a particular number of items, say exactly two. For that specify two '
 'integers `(min max)` rather than a Boolean as second positional parameter:'
 ''
 '```   '
 '      caption (2 2) CommTools.Select l  '
 '```'
 ''
 'The user must then select at least `min` and at most `max` items, so `(2 2)` means "exactly two" while '
 '`(2 3)` means "two or three". Both must be integers between 1 and the number of choices, and `min` must '
 'not be greater than `max`.'
 ''
 'Note that with `(min max)` the keywords "a" and "a~{n}" are not on offer.'
 ''
 'If you want to force the user to make a selection, pass a 1 as third positional parameter: '
 ''
 '```   '
 '      caption 1 1 CommTools.Select l  '
 '```'
 (' '⋄)
 'This prevents the user from pressing `<Enter>` without making any input, and by default also from entering'
 '"q" (for quit). `mustFlag` keeps its meaning when `manyFlag` is `(min max)`: `caption (2 2) 1` demands exactly'
 'two items and does not allow the user to quit.'
 ''
 'Sometimes a selection is required, but the user should still be able to cancel the whole operation. For that'
 'pass a 1 as fourth positional parameter (`quitFlag`):'
 ''
 '```'
 '      caption 1 1 1 CommTools.Select l'
 '```'
 ''
 'The user must then either select at least one item or enter "q", in which case ¯1 is returned.'
 ''
 '`quitFlag` defaults to `~mustFlag`, so leaving it out keeps the behaviour described above. You may also pass'
 'a 0 together with a `mustFlag` of 0: the user may then select nothing, but cannot quit. Like `mustFlag`,'
 '`quitFlag` keeps its meaning when `manyFlag` is `(min max)`.'
 ''
 'The caption must be a simple character vector without line feeds or carriage returns.'
 ''
 '#### Specifying a selection for automation'
 ''
 'The general principles of automation have been discussed earlier, but in one respect `Select` is different:'
 ''
 'If you need a specific selection to be returned but you don''t know the item number in advance, because the list is '
 'created dynamically, you can specify the selection itself instead of an integer.'
 ''
 'For example:'
 ''
 '```'
 '      CommTools.AddAutomation ''Select'' ''My caption'' ''Second'''
 '      ⍞←''My caption'' CommTools.Select l'
 '2     '
 '```'
 ''
 'Altogether these are the values you may specify as the result of a `Select` automation:'
 ''
 '| Value              | Effect                                                         |'
 '|:-------------------|:---------------------------------------------------------------|'
 '| An integer         | The number of the item to be selected                          |'
 '| A vector of them   | The numbers of the items to be selected                        |'
 '| `0` or `⍬`         | Quit without selecting anything                                |'
 '| `''q''`              | Quit without selecting anything                                |'
 '| `''a''`              | Select all items                                               |'
 '| `''a~3 5''`          | Select all items but 3 and 5                                   |'
 '| Any other text     | The item that matches the text; `*` acts as wildcard, see below|'
 ''
 'Note that `''a''` and `''a~3 5''` are accepted no matter whether `manyFlag` is 1 or not: automation is under your'
 'control, so `CommTools` does not get in your way here. When `mustFlag` is 1, however, `''a~...''` must not'
 'exclude every single item, because that would leave nothing selected.'
 ''
 'For the same reason `manyFlag` does not restrict automation, and that includes the `(min max)` syntax: '
 'you may specify any number of items, no matter what the user would be allowed to select.'
 ''
 'Nor does `quitFlag` restrict automation: `''q''` returns ¯1 no matter whether the user would be allowed to quit.'
 ''
 'Note that ranges like `''3-6''` are not available for automation: such a value is matched against the text of'
 'the items, like any other text. Specify a vector of item numbers instead, for example `2+⍳4`. After `a~`,'
 'however, ranges are accepted: `''a~3-6''` selects all items but 3 to 6.'
 ''
 '#### Using a wildcard character'
 ''
 'It might be difficult or even impossible to specify the full text of the item you want to select, typically'
 'because the list is created dynamically.'
 ''
 'In such a case use the `*` glyph as a wildcard character. It must be either the **first** or the **last** '
 'character of the value; anywhere else it is taken literally, and no match will be found.'
 ''
 'Examples:'
 ''
 'I. `*` at the end: the value must match the **beginning** of an item'
 ''
 '```'
 '      list←''First item'' ''Second item'' ''Third item'''
 '      CommTools.AddAutomation ''Select'' ''My caption'' ''Second*'''
 '      ''My caption'' CommTools.Select list'
 ('2'⋄)
 '```'
 ''
 'II. `*` at the beginning: the value must match the **end** of an item'
 ''
 '```'
 '      list←''/path2/file_1'' ''/path2/file_2'' ''/path2/file_3'''
 '      CommTools.AddAutomation ''Select'' ''My caption'' ''*_3'''
 '      ''My caption'' CommTools.Select list'
 ('3'⋄)
 '```'
 ''
 'If a wildcard matches more than one item, all matching item numbers are returned.'
 ''
 '### `Pause` '
 ''
 'The purpose of this function is to print a message to the session, possibly with line feed characters '
 '(`⎕UCS 10`) in it, and tell the user to press `<enter>` in order to continue.'
 ''
 '```'
 '      CommTools.Pause ''Make sure you are connected to the Internet'''
 '```'
 ''
 'By passing a 1 as left argument (`lineFlag`) a horizontal line is printed above the message. Its length is'
 'defined by `⎕PW-1`.'
 ''
 'The function has a shy result: a 1 in case it did present the message and stopped, and a 0 in case automation'
 'prevented that.'
 ''
 'Like the other functions, you can interrupt `Pause` by typing `∘∘∘` and then pressing `<enter>`.'
 ''
 '#### `Pause` and automation'
 ''
 'The general principles of automation have been discussed earlier, but in one respect `Pause` is different:'
 ''
 'If you don''t want your program/user command to pause at all, use the wildcard symbol (`*`) as ID:'
 ''
 '```'
 '      CommTools.AddAutomation ''Pause'' ''*'' '''''
 '```'
 ''
 'This is interpreted as "don''t stop when `Pause` is called anywhere".'
 ''
 'This holds even when there are other `Pause` rows. If one of them matches the message or its alias, the counter'
 'of that row is increased; otherwise the counter of the `*` row is.'
 ''
 '### `AskForNumber`'
 ''
 'Imagine you ask this question:'
 ''
 '```'
 '      ({1}CommTools.AskForNumber) ''Number of copies to print'''
 '```'
 ''
 'Note that the operator `AskForNumber` requires a check function to be passed as left operand. In the example '
 'the check function simply returns a 1. Instead you could perform a real check like this:'
 ''
 '```'
 'CheckFn←{'
 '   ⍵∊1 2 3 4 5:1 '
 '   ⍞←''Please enter a number between 1 and 5'' '
 '   0'
 ('}'⋄)
 '      (CheckFn CommTools.AskForNumber) ''Number of copies to print:'''
 '```     '
 ''
 'The check function gets the number entered by the user as right argument, and is expected to return a 1 if the'
 'input is acceptable and a 0 if it is not. In the latter case the user is asked again.'
 ''
 'If the user does not enter a number but just presses `<enter>`, an empty vector is returned. You can force the '
 'user to enter a number by passing a 1 as left argument (`enforce`).'
 ''
 'Note that leading spaces in the question are removed, and that a colon is appended unless the question ends'
 'with one already.'
 ''
 '### `AskForText`'
 ''
 'Imagine you are asking this question:'
 ''
 '```'
 '      ({1}CommTools.AskForText) ''Enter a month (three characters)'''
 '```'
 ''
 'Note that the operator `AskForText` requires a check function to be passed as left operand. In the example '
 'the check function simply returns a 1. Instead you could perform a real check like this:'
 ''
 '```'
 'CheckFn←{'
 '   (⊂⍵)∊↓12 3⍴''janfebmaraprmayjunjulaugsepoctnovdec'':1 '
 '   ⍞←''Please enter just three lowercase characters identifying a month'' '
 '   0'
 ('}'⋄)
 '      (CheckFn CommTools.AskForText) ''Enter a month (just three characters)'''
 '```     '
 ''
 'The check function gets the text entered by the user as right argument, and is expected to return a 1 if the'
 'input is acceptable and a 0 if it is not. In the latter case the user is asked again.'
 ''
 'If the user does not enter anything but just hits `<enter>` an empty vector will be returned. '
 'You can force the user to enter something by passing a 1 as left argument (`enforce`).'
 ''
 'Instead of a Boolean you can also specify a character vector as `⍺`. In that case, when the user just presses '
 '`<enter>` without entering anything, the default (`⍺`) is returned. The default is shown to the user in square'
 'brackets as part of the question.'
 ''
 'Note that leading spaces in the question are removed, and that a colon is appended unless the question ends'
 'with one already.'
 ''
 '## Helpers'
 ''
 'There are three helpers available that assist the user in dealing with the `∆Automation` variable.'
 ''
 '### `AddAutomation`'
 ''
 'This allows you to add a row to the variable `∆Automation`. If `∆Automation` does not exist it''s created.'
 ''
 'The function requires 3 values as right argument:'
 ''
 '1. The type: must be one of `AskForNumber`, `AskForText`, `Pause`, `Select` or `YesOrNo`'
 '2. The ID: either a message/question/caption or an alias; must not be empty'
 '3. The data to be returned, if any '
 ''
 'For two of the types the data is checked:'
 ''
 '* For `AskForNumber` it must be numeric'
 '* For `YesOrNo` it must be empty or one of "Y", "y", "N" and "n" --- an empty value makes `YesOrNo` return'
 '  its default'
 ''
 'If the ID is already defined for the given type, the user is asked whether the already existing row should'
 'really be overwritten. If she answers "no" no action is taken. You can enforce the update, and therefore avoid'
 'the question, by passing a 1 as left argument (`overwrite`).'
 ''
 'The function has a shy result:'
 ''
 '* `0` in case no action was taken'
 '* `1` in case an already existing row was updated (which also resets the counter)'
 '* `2` in case a new row was added'
 ''
 '### `ListAutomation`'
 ''
 'This function requires a right argument: when an empty vector is provided, then all entries in `∆Automation` are'
 'returned. If it is not empty it is treated as a list of types, for example:'
 ''
 '```'
 '      ListAutomation ''Select'''
 '      ListAutomation ''Select'' ''YesOrNo'' '
 '```'
 ''
 'By default column headers are added, and a message (character vector) is returned in case `∆Automation` '
 'does not exist or is empty or has no data for the specified type(s).'
 ''
 'This can be changed by specifying a 1 as left argument (`rawFlag`): then a four-column matrix without titles is'
 'returned. In case there is no data to be reported that matrix is empty, so a program can process the result'
 'without having to check for a message first.'
 ''
 '### `FindAutomationIndex`'
 ''
 'This function takes `type` and `id` as right argument and returns one of:'
 ''
 '* `⍬` in case `∆Automation` does not exist or no matching row was found '
 '* The index of the row with a match'
 ''
 'Therefore it can also be used to determine whether a certain alias is defined in `∆Automation`, see this example:'
 ''
 '```'
 '      ⍬≢FindAutomationIndex ''YesOrNo'' ''Sure@''   ⍝ Does this exist?'
 ('1'⋄)
 '```'
 ''
 '## Other functions'
 ''
 '| Function      | Purpose                                                                          |'
 '|:--------------|:---------------------------------------------------------------------------------|'
 '| `Cleanup`     | Removes `∆Automation`, see [Cleaning up](#)                                      |'
 '| `Help`        | Puts this document on display; same as `]ADoc CommTools`                         |'
 '| `Public`      | Returns the names that make up the public interface of `CommTools`               |'
 '| `SetOnWait`   | Establishes a function that is called before waiting for input, see [Getting notified](#) |'
 '| `UnsetOnWait` | Removes the function established by `SetOnWait`, see [Getting notified](#)       |'
 ''
)
